<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Description>Preview, not production ready. Microsoft Agent Framework (MAF) adapter for AgentExperience.NET: one AIAgentBuilder.UseExperienceCapture(...) call records every MAF invocation (ordinary, streaming, failed, cancelled, or abandoned mid-stream) and its tool calls as a sanitized Experience Run through AgentExperience.Core's IExperienceCaptureService. Requires Microsoft.Agents.AI 1.22.0 or any later 1.x.</Description>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- A tested range, not a bare floor (story 7.2, KL-13): 1.22.0 up to, but not including, 2.0.0. The floor is
         what the committed lock files resolve and every test runs; the newest 1.x is proven by CI's MAF probe
         (latest leg) and floating-dependency leg, which both gate pushes and the weekly schedule. The upper bound
         is MAF's next major: MAF states no SemVer promise and shipped five caller-visible behaviour changes between
         1.15 and 1.22, so a major is where a break is expected, and a host on it is told (NU1608) rather than
         silently run against an adapter nobody built for it. A newer 1.x minor restores with no warning.
         docs/compatibility-evidence.md has the evidence. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Agents.AI" Version="[1.22.0, 2.0.0)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\AgentExperience.Core\AgentExperience.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="AgentExperience.MicrosoftAgentFramework.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
